  },
  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      83.78,
      208.25,
      515.49,
      329.69
    ],
    "text": "It was held in W.A Fernando Vs. The Queen 76 NLR 265 that though a prosecutor is not bound to expose every infirmity and weakness in his case, yet when a person is brought up on a capital charge, and there is some item of evidence which C3$ts some serious doubts on his guilt, it is the duty of the prosecutor to draw the attention of the trial Judge to such evidence.",
    "fonts": [
      "Helvetica",
      "Times-Roman"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      14.036713600158691,
      14.566734313964844,
      14.767642974853516,
      14.96585464477539,
      15.100000381469727,
      15.145182609558105,
      15.213821411132812,
      15.356643676757812,
      15.610036849975586,
      15.744016647338867
    ]
  },
  {
    "page_number": 3,
    "bbox": [
      83.15,
      339.29,
      520.0,
      440.31
    ],
    "text": "At this juncture it is pertinent to refer to rule 52 of the Supreme Court Rules which provides thus: \"It shall be the duty of an Attorney-At-Law appearing for the prosecution to bring to the notice of the Court or Tribunal any matter if withheld may lead to a misearriage -of--justice.\"",
    "fonts": [
      "Helvetica",
      "Times-Roman"
    ],
    "sizes": [
      14.21469783782959,
      14.5,
      14.767642974853516,
      15.029837608337402,
      15.033865928649902,
      15.041988372802734,
      15.100000381469727,
      15.261487007141113,
      15.474847793579102,
      15.557374000549316,
      15.610036849975586,
      15.744016647338867
    ]
  },
